      "text": "Rewrite the given line equation as\n\\[4y = 3x - 5,\\]or\n\\[3x - 4y = 5.\\]Note that the origin is not on the line when $x = 0$ and $y = 0.$  Substitute $x = 3$ into $3x - 4y = 5$ to find $y = -\\frac{1}{4}.$  Therefore, the point $(3, -\\frac{1}{4})$ is on the line, and the direction vector $\\mathbf{d} - \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} x - 3 \\\\ y + \\frac{1}{4} \\end{pmatrix} = t \\mathbf{d}.$  Hence, $\\mathbf{d} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix},$ so $\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}}.$The answer is: \\begin{pmatrix}4\\-3\\end{pmatrix}",
