      "text": "To parameterize the line \\( y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4} \\) in the form \\( \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{v} + t \\mathbf{d} \\), we need to find a point on the line and a direction vector that satisfies the given conditions.\n\nFirst, we need to find a point on the line. Let's choose \\( x = 3 \\). Substituting \\( x = 3 \\) into the equation of the line, we get:\n\\[ y = \\frac{3(3) - 5}{4} = \\frac{9 - 5}{4} = \\frac{4}{4} = 1. \\]\nSo, the point \\((3, 1)\\) lies on the line. This point will serve as the point \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) in the parameterization.\n\nNext, we need to find the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\). The slope of the line is 3, so the direction vector can be taken as \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\). However, we need to check if this direction vector satisfies the condition that the distance between \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is \\( t \\).\n\nThe distance formula between \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is:\n\\[ \\sqrt{(x - 3)^2 + (y - 1)^2}. \\]\nWe know that this distance is \\( t \\), so we have:\n\\[ \\sqrt{(x - 3)^2 + (y - 1)^2} = t. \\]\nSubstituting \\( y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4} \\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[ \\sqrt{(x - 3)^2 + \\left(\\frac{3x - 5}{4} - 1\\right)^2} = t. \\]\nSimplifying the expression inside the square root:\n\\[ \\frac{3x - 5}{4} - 1 = \\frac{3x - 5 - 4}{4} = \\frac{3x - 9}{4}. \\]\nSo the equation becomes:\n\\[ \\sqrt{(x - 3)^2 + \\left(\\frac{3x - 9}{4}\\right)^2} = t. \\]\nSimplifying further:\n\\[ \\sqrt{(x - 3)^2 + \\frac{(3x - 9)^2}{16}} = t, \\]\n\\[ \\sqrt{(x - 3)^2 + \\frac{9(x - 3)^2}{16}} = t, \\]\n\\[ \\sqrt{(x - 3)^2 \\left(1 + \\frac{9}{16}\\right)} = t, \\]\n\\[ \\sqrt{(x - 3)^2 \\left(\\frac{25}{16}\\right)} = t, \\]\n\\[ \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{5(x - 3)}{4}\\right)^2} = t, \\]\n\\[ \\frac{5|x - 3|}{4} = t. \\]\nThis confirms that the distance is indeed \\( t \\), and the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nTherefore, the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}}\\).",
