      "text": "We start by identifying the given line in the parameterized form:\n\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{v} + t \\mathbf{d},\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) is a point on the line and \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is the direction vector of the line. The line is given by the equation:\n\n\\[y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4}.\\]\n\nTo find the point \\(\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{pmatrix} x_0 \\\\ y_0 \\end{pmatrix}\\) on the line, we can choose a convenient value for \\(x\\), say \\(x_0 = 3\\):\n\n\\[y_0 = \\frac{3(3) - 5}{4} = \\frac{9 - 5}{4} = \\frac{4}{4} = 1.\\]\n\nThus, the point \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Now, we need to find the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) such that the distance between \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is \\(t\\) for \\(x \\ge 3\\).\n\nThe general point on the line can be written as:\n\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} d_1 \\\\ d_2 \\end{pmatrix},\\]\n\nor equivalently,\n\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 + td_1 \\\\ 1 + td_2 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n\nThe distance from \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 + td_1 \\\\ 1 + td_2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\\sqrt{(3 + td_1 - 3)^2 + (1 + td_2 - 1)^2} = \\sqrt{(td_1)^2 + (td_2)^2} = t\\sqrt{d_1^2 + d_2^2}.\\]\n\nWe equate this to \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\\sqrt{d_1^2 + d_2^2} = 1.\\]\n\nSquaring both sides, we get:\n\n\\[d_1^2 + d_2^2 = 1.\\]\n\nSince we are only interested in the direction vector and not its length, we can choose \\(\\mathbf{d} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), which satisfies \\(d_1^2 + d_2^2 = 4^2 + 3^2 = 16 + 9 = 25 \\neq 1\\) but does not affect the distance calculation as we are not concerned with its length. The correct direction vector is:\n\n\\[\\mathbf{d} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n\nThus, 